Build for the architecture baseline, not for the build machine's CPU

build.zig hands the -Dtarget string straight to std.Target.Query.parse, and
the string "native" leaves cpu_model at .determined_by_arch_os, which for a
native query means "detect this CPU and enable everything it has".

That is right for a developer building for themselves and wrong for a
distribution, where the build worker and the user are different machines. On
an aarch64 worker with FEAT_LRCPC (ARMv8.3) the C++ in yoga compiles down to
ldapr/stlur, and libopentui.so then dies with SIGILL on any older aarch64 -
ThunderX2, Cortex-A72 and friends - the moment yoga lays out its first node.
The x86_64 side has the same shape: whatever the worker happens to support
gets baked in.

Pin the CPU model to the architecture baseline instead. This only affects
which instructions the code generator may use; the libc and libstdc++ the
library links against still come from the host, so the rest of the native
build is unchanged. It also matches how upstream builds the artifacts it
publishes to npm, since every entry in SUPPORTED_TARGETS is an explicit
triple and an explicit triple already resolves to the baseline model.

Not sent upstream: -Dtarget=native meaning "this exact machine" is a
reasonable default for the audience upstream is building for.

Index: opentui-0.4.5/packages/core/src/zig/build.zig
===================================================================
--- opentui-0.4.5.orig/packages/core/src/zig/build.zig
+++ opentui-0.4.5/packages/core/src/zig/build.zig
@@ -466,7 +466,11 @@ fn buildTarget(
     build_options: *std.Build.Step.Options,
     macos_sdk_path: ?[]const u8,
 ) !void {
-    const target_query = try std.Target.Query.parse(.{ .arch_os_abi = zig_target });
+    var target_query = try std.Target.Query.parse(.{ .arch_os_abi = zig_target });
+    // Never generate for the build machine's CPU: a shipped library has to
+    // run on the oldest CPU the architecture defines, not on the newest one
+    // that happened to compile it.
+    target_query.cpu_model = .baseline;
     const target = b.resolveTargetQuery(target_query);

     if (target.result.os.tag == .macos and macos_sdk_path == null) {
